The lemmas below apply to the **module as written**: `S1`, `S2`, and `itfc_state` are one-bit registers, so assigned multi-bit state values are truncated. They assume `prop` is checked at `@(posedge clock)`, since its declaration has no clock. ([fpga.mit.edu](https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clock) (send_data == 1'b0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "S1 can only be 0 or 1. Its reachable case branches never set send_data, which is initialized to 0."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clock) ((S2 == `GP01) && (shot == 1'b0)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "With send_data always 0, S2 remains in GP01. It cannot reach a branch that raises shot."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clock) ((itfc_state == `G_IDLE) && (send == 1'b0)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "With shot always 0, the interface remains idle and never raises send. Its idle branch clears confirm each cycle, making prop inductive regardless of send_en."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

I’ll check the one-bit state encodings and look for inductive invariants that connect `confirm` and `send_en`.<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clock) (send_data == 1'b0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "S1 is declared as one bit. Wider state assignments truncate to that width, so only the GP001 and GP010 case branches execute; neither sets send_data. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/eda/sv-bc/10291.html?utm_source=openai))"
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clock) (S2 == `GP01 && shot == 1'b0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Given lemma_1, S2 remains in GP01. It cannot reach the GP10 branch that asserts shot."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clock) (itfc_state == `G_IDLE && confirm == 1'b0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Given lemma_2, shot remains low, so the interface stays in G_IDLE and clears confirm each cycle. This invariant implies prop regardless of send_en."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

As declared, `S1`, `S2`, and `itfc_state` are **one-bit registers**. Assignments of wider state codes are truncated, so this proof follows the reachable behavior of the code as written, not the apparent intended FSMs. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/eda/sv-bc/10291.html?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clock) (send_data == 1'b0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "S1 can hold only GP001 or GP010. It therefore cannot reach GP111, the only state that sets send_data."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clock) (S2 == `GP01); endproperty",
      "explanation": "S2 starts in GP01 and, with send_data always zero, remains there. This lemma uses lemma_1."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clock) (shot == 1'b0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Shot starts at zero and is not changed in GP01. This lemma uses lemma_2."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_4; @(posedge clock) (itfc_state == `G_IDLE); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The interface starts idle and cannot leave while shot is zero. In G_IDLE, every clock clears confirm, making the target property 1-inductive with these lemmas."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

To check `prop`, assert it on `posedge clock`; its declaration alone does not run a check. ([eda.org](https://www.eda.org/images/resources/videos/SystemVerilog_Assertions_Tutorial_2016.pdf?utm_source=openai))

The key is the declared width: `itfc_state` is **one bit**. Assigning it `G_SEND` (`2'b10`) truncates the value to zero, and the `G_WAIT_END` case cannot match. That makes the `G_LOAD` phase a useful invariant boundary. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/eda/sv-bc/10291.html?utm_source=openai))

